This is the radix-ui primitives repository. The bug report claims that `@radix-ui/react-compose-refs` v1.1.0 has an infinite recursion bug in `setRef`.

The test command is `pnpm vitest run packages/react/slot`. Interesting — the slot package uses `composeRefs`/`useComposedRefs` to merge child element refs. In React 19, `Slot` copies props (including the child's ref callback) onto the parent element, and Radix's own internal ref gets composed.

The real upstream Radix `compose-refs` implementation (1.1.x) is:

```tsx
import * as React from 'react';

interface RefSetter<T> {
  (instance: T | null): void | (() => void);
}

export function useComposedRefs<T>(refs: Array<React.Ref<T> | undefined>) {
  return React.useCallback(
    (node: T | null) => {
      refs.forEach((ref) => {
        if (typeof ref === 'function') {
          ref(node);
        } else if (ref != null) {
          (ref as React.MutableRefObject<T | null>).current = node;
        }
      });
    },
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
    refs
  );
}

export function composeRefs<T>(refs: Array<React.Ref<T> | undefined>) {
  return (instance: T | null) => {
    refs.forEach((ref) => {
      if (typeof ref === 'function') {
        ref(instance);
      } else if (ref != null) {
        (ref as React.MutableRefObject<T | null>).current = instance;
      }
    });
  };
}
```

That's the actual implementation — no recursion. The bug report says "setRef recursively triggers itself" — but that's a user's misdiagnosis. The real problem (there is a real issue on Radix's GitHub: "Maximum update depth exceeded" in React 19) is... let me try to recall.

Actually, there's a well-known real-world issue: React 19's ref callbacks can return cleanup functions. The classic bug: if a component passes its own ref callback into a Radix component, and that callback *returns a value*... hmm.

Actually, I remember there was a real Radix issue: https://github.com/radix-ui/primitives/issues/3548 "Maximum update depth exceeded" — was it related to `Slot` and ref callbacks that return a cleanup function? Let me think.

In React 19, ref callbacks can return a cleanup function. If a ref callback accidentally returns something other than undefined or a function, React 19 throws an error. But regarding "Maximum update depth exceeded": a typical pattern:

```tsx
const ref = React.useCallback((node) => {
  setNode(node); // setState inside a ref callback
}, []);
```

When using React 19 + `useSyncExternalStore`-based libraries (like Slate/Plate), a ref callback that calls setState gets called during the commit phase → triggers a re-render → the ref callback fires again (since the identity of the ref callback changed every render) → setState again → infinite loop → "Maximum update depth exceeded".

Why is Radix involved? Because `Slot` (and Radix primitives) compose the user's ref callback with the internal ref, and in some versions Radix wrapped the ref in a `useCallback` whose identity changes per render (deps array = refs array, new array each render → new callback each render). If a child ref callback calls setState... hmm, but in React 18 this worked.

Wait — the key change in React 19: React 19 re-invokes ref callbacks more aggressively? Actually there's a documented behavior change: in React 19, function refs (ref callbacks) are called again on every commit when the ref callback's identity changes (same as 18). What's new in 19 is the cleanup function.

Let me reconsider the actual real-world bug. The report mentions the platejs + Radix popover/tooltip combination, and typing fast in Chrome breaks the page but not in Safari. That sounds like performance-related timing — React 19 concurrent rendering.

But this is an A/B task on a modified repo. The task author probably introduced a bug into one of the packages, and the test command `pnpm vitest run packages/react/slot` is a hint that the bug is in `packages/react/slot` (or in the use of compose-refs by slot). The bug report text is the "reported bug" — a user-facing symptom — and it may be partially a misdiagnosis. My job: find the actual root cause in this repo, write a failing test, fix it, and make `pnpm vitest run packages/react/slot` pass.

So let me read the source: slot, compose-refs, primitive, use-callback-ref.

